            title: 'Pyramide',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Pyramide (The Pyramid) is a popular French social card game, often played in festive or party settings. It is a game of memory, deduction, and high-stakes bluffing. Players are dealt a private hand of cards which they must mÃ©morise and then place face-down. A central 'Pyramid' of cards is then revealed layer-by-layer. If a player claims to have a card matching a revealed one, they can penalize an opponentâ€”but if they're caught bluffing, the penalty is doubled. It is a game of 'Who can keep a poker face while forgetting their own hand?'",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Likely developed in the late 20th century as a simplified version of more complex betting games. It is part of a family of 'Social Penalty' games (Jeux Ã  boire) common in European university culture.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "4-6 is the 'Sweet spot' for memory tension.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52 cards.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52 cards.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player receives 4 cards face-down. They have 10 seconds to mÃ©morise them and then cannot look at them again.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
