    historyContent: {
      origins: "Likely developed in the late 20th century as a simplified version of more complex betting games. It is part of a family of 'Social Penalty' games (Jeux Ã  boire) common in European university culture.",
      originCountries: [
        'France',
        'Belgium',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "1980s: Emerges in French student bars as a 'Filler' game.",
        "1990s: Rules for the 'Double-Penalty' bluff are standardized.",
        "2010s: Becomes a staple of 'Appero' culture (social pre-drinks) in France and Belgium.",
        'Present: One of the most-played communal games for its simplicity and immediate social tension.',
      ],
      evolution: "The transition from counting points to 'Drinking Penalties' (gorgÃ©es) made the physical stakes of the game more immediate and humorous for social groups.",
      cultural: "Associated with the phrase 'C'est pour qui?' (Who is this for?) as players decide which friend to target with a revealed card.",
